{"authored" [{"question" "q-351832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x)$ be a function with $f(2)=5,{f}^{′}(2)=3,{f}^{″}(2)=4,$ and ${f}^{‴}(2)=-6.$ What is the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}(x)$ for $f(x)$ about $x=2?$\n\n${P}_{3}(x)=5\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$(x-2)\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-2)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-2)^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "4780fb59f98b666bfc992f36488f94cbd2c11d9e6dc9df3662a452e10101ef69" "basis" 1305 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(2)}{2!}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(2)}{3!}(x-2)^{3} \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(2)}{2}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(2)}{6}(x-2)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have been given the following set of values: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(2) & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our Taylor polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =5+(3)\\cdot (x-2)+\\frac{4}{2}\\cdot (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{6}\\cdot (x-2)^{3} \\\\  & =5+3\\,(x-2)+2\\,(x-2)^{2}-1\\,(x-2)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/state.json" "file_sha256" "4780fb59f98b666bfc992f36488f94cbd2c11d9e6dc9df3662a452e10101ef69" "basis" 1305 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038458/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351832" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/ho